#X text 240 83 Joins two envelopes into a single one: the second envelope
is appended after the first \, so the resulting envelope contains the
points of the first envelope followed by the points of the second.
The two envelopes can have different segment types and curve shapes.;
#X text 240 168 Typical use cases:;
#X text 250 193 • Building long envelopes from short building blocks
— for example \, chaining an ADSR attack phase with an LFO-like decay
segment or a long release tail.;
#X text 250 248 • Multi-stage shapes — creating envelopes with more
than the traditional ADSR stages \, such as attack–decay–hold–decay–release
\, by concatenating several short envelopes.;
#X text 250 318 • Complex modulation shapes — assembling an envelope
that combines several different behaviors over time \, for example
a slow rise followed by a fast wobble followed by a gentle fade-out.;
